# .trivyignore — waivers for Trivy findings you have reviewed and accepted.
#
# Add one CVE ID per line. Comments above the ID are part of the file's git
# history and become your audit trail for why a finding is not being fixed.
#
# Example:
#
#   # CVE-2025-12345 — libcurl in upstream base. Not reachable from our
#   # deployment (container runs behind mTLS ingress, no outbound HTTP).
#   # Revisit 2026-08 when upstream bumps the base image.
#   CVE-2025-12345
#
# Leave this file empty (except for comments) until you actually need a waiver.
# A waiver without a reason is just a mute button, not evidence.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CVE-2026-4800 — lodash _.template code injection / prototype pollution.
#   lodash < 4.18.0, fixed in 4.18.0. GHSA-r5fr-rjxr-66jc. The CVE-2021-23337
#   fix validated the `variable` option of _.template but not the
#   `options.imports` key names; an attacker who controls those keys can
#   inject code.
#
# Where it surfaces: a transitive npm dependency of the hub frontend
#   (internal/site, bun.lock / package-lock.json). Flagged by the Trivy *fs*
#   scan of the cloned source, NOT the image scan — the runtime image is
#   scratch + Go binary with the frontend embedded as minified client-side
#   JS, so there is no lodash lockfile/package in the published image.
#
# Why waived, not fixed:
#   1. Mirror policy. We do not override upstream's lockfiles — bumping lodash
#      is henrygd's call. Pinning a different version here would diverge our
#      build from upstream behaviour, which is out of scope for a mirror.
#      (Same stance as the mirror-uptime-kuma npm-layer waivers.)
#   2. Sink not reached in a server-exploitable way. lodash here is bundled
#      client-side frontend code; the _.template imports-key sink requires the
#      application to call _.template with attacker-controlled `imports` keys,
#      which is not how the Beszel dashboard uses it. Not a server-side RCE in
#      the hub.
#
# Action: renewed at v0.19.0 — still 4.17.21 in internal/site/bun.lock, so the
# finding stands. Note upstream is mid-migration between lockfiles: at v0.19.0
# internal/site/package-lock.json already carries lodash 4.18.1 (fixed) while
# bun.lock — the lockfile our frontend stage actually installs from
# (`bun install --frozen-lockfile`) — still resolves 4.17.21. Regenerating
# bun.lock is henrygd's call and will clear this on its own. Revisit 2026-12-01.
CVE-2026-4800

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build-stage frontend tooling — js-yaml and browserslist.
#
#   CVE-2026-59869      js-yaml 4.1.1  → 4.3.0   Quadratic CPU parsing merge-key
#                                                chains. DoS.
#   GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj js-yaml 4.1.1  → 4.3.1   O(n²) `!!omap` key-uniqueness
#                                                scan in resolveYamlOmap(). DoS.
#                                                (CVE-2026-59870 fix, not
#                                                backported to the 4.1.x line.)
#   CVE-2026-73088      browserslist 4.25.1 → 4.28.7  normalizeStats() walks
#                                                untrusted stats data with an
#                                                unguarded for..in → prototype
#                                                pollution / uncaught TypeError.
#   CVE-2026-73089      browserslist 4.25.1 → 4.28.7  Unbounded query/AST cache,
#                                                no TTL or eviction → linear
#                                                memory growth → OOM.
#
# Where they surface: the Trivy *fs* scan of the cloned upstream source
#   (internal/site/package-lock.json and bun.lock). Both are transitive
#   build-time tooling — js-yaml via cosmiconfig (config-file loader),
#   browserslist via @babel/helper-compilation-targets and
#   update-browserslist-db.
#
# Why waived, not fixed:
#   1. Not present in the published image. The runtime is `scratch` + one
#      static Go binary + CA certs. There is no JS runtime, no node_modules
#      and no lockfile in the image — the frontend ships as minified assets
#      embedded in the binary via `go:embed`. These packages exist only inside
#      the throwaway bun builder stage. The image scan does not and cannot
#      flag them; only the source scan sees them.
#   2. No attacker-controlled input at the sink. All four require input the
#      attacker influences — untrusted YAML for js-yaml, and
#      browserslist-stats.json / opts.stats / repeated query values for
#      browserslist. In our pipeline these tools read only upstream's own
#      committed config inside a hermetic CI build of a pinned tag. The worst
#      realistic case is a DoS against our own build job — an availability
#      issue for the mirror, not a property of the artifact we publish.
#   3. Mirror policy. We do not override upstream's lockfiles; bumping these
#      is henrygd's call. Pinning different versions here would diverge our
#      build from upstream behaviour, which is out of scope for a mirror.
#      (Same stance as the mirror-uptime-kuma npm-layer waivers.)
#
# Action: revisit 2026-12-01, or sooner when upstream regenerates the frontend
# lockfiles — at which point these lines should be removed and the findings
# will clear on their own.
CVE-2026-59869
GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj
CVE-2026-73088
CVE-2026-73089
